{"qid":"Q240123","names":["Indo-Scythians"],"title":"Indo-Scythians","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Indo-Scythians were Iranian-speaking Saka nomads who displaced the Indo-Greek kingdom in the northwestern Indian subcontinent from roughly the late second century BCE onward, establishing satrapal rule over Gandhara, the Punjab, Sindh, and eventually much of western India. Their greatest ruler, Azes I (fl. c. 58–12 BCE), whose reign defines the proposed 'Azes Era' chronology, consolidated control over Taxila and issued extensive bilingual coinage attesting Greek and Kharoshthi administrative usage. Indo-Scythian power fragmented in the first and second centuries CE as the Kushan Empire expanded from the northwest and the Western Satraps asserted independence in Gujarat and Rajasthan, with the last Indo-Scythian rulers disappearing from the numismatic record by the late second or early third century CE. The polity is attested in the dataset through 282 CE, reflecting the residual Western Satrapal horizon.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is reconstructed primarily from coin hoards, dynastic sequences, and a handful of inscriptions; specific frontier limits in the Deccan and Gangetic plain are conjectural, and the chronology of individual rulers remains debated owing to conflicting numismatic evidence.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Indo-Scythians","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indo-Scythians"},{"label":"Britannica: Kushan dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Kushan-dynasty"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Indo-Scythians","rulers":[{"qid":